.TH MU2E-POWER-NETCHECK 1 "2026-09-29" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-power-netcheck \- phase 3: inter-node connectivity across the DAQ networks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-power-netcheck
.RB [ \-\-include\-failed ]
.RB [ \-\-location
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-node
.IR HOST ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs phase 3 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
.PP
Phases 1 and 2 establish that each node is reachable
.I from a gateway.
That is a weaker property than the nodes being able to reach
.IR "each other" .
After a power event a switch can come back with a VLAN missing, a port in the
wrong group, or jumbo frames disabled on one uplink, and every node still
passes its own health checks while the DAQ cannot move data. This phase probes
node to node.
.PP
The networks probed, and how, come from the
.I mesh:
section of
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5):
.TP
.B data
Full mesh, with a jumbo-frame probe. This is the network the DAQ actually uses
and it is small enough to afford O(N^2) probes.
.TP
.B lab
Sampled: each node is probed against a small set of anchor hosts, which is O(N)
and sufficient to find a segment that has not come back.
.TP
.B ipmi
Probed from the gateways, not from the nodes. A node's
.I ipmi
entry is its BMC, on a private subnet that only the gateways route to (which is
why
.BR ipmitool
runs there), so a node's own OS cannot ping it on a healthy network. Each of a
location's gateways pings every BMC of the probed nodes in that location, and
the result names the gateway as the source. What this proves: every BMC answers
ICMP from the hosts that phase 2 will drive it from. It does not prove IPMI
authentication works, and it says nothing about host-to-BMC reachability, which
the design does not need.
.PP
Probes for one source are packed into a single remote shell invocation rather
than one SSH session per pair \(em the shipped topology has 49 nodes on the data
network, so the mesh is 2352 ordered pairs, and doing that as 2352 sessions
would take longer than the rest of the recovery. As one session per source it
is 49.
.PP
The results are interpreted, not merely tabulated. A node that reached nothing
is reported separately from a node that nobody could reach: the first is a node
or switch-port problem, the second suggests a one-way path, an ARP problem or a
host firewall, and they need different responses.
.PP
FAIL and UNKNOWN are kept apart. A path is FAIL when the ping ran and got no
reply, when the target name does not resolve on the source, or when the source
has no route to it; it is UNKNOWN when it was never tested \(em the source
could not be logged into, the probe raised, the output had no result for that
target, or ping printed no statistics summary because it never ran
.RI ( "ping: not found" ,
.IR "Operation not permitted" ,
a BusyBox ping rejecting
.BR "\-M do" ).
Such a path's detail quotes ping's first line. The same applies to the
jumbo-frame probe: one that produced no summary leaves the path's MTU result
unset rather than failed.
A source that tested nothing is reported as an unreachable source, and its
paths are left out of the isolated-node and unreachable-target analysis, so an
SSH failure on one source is not presented as a broken switch. On the
.B ipmi
network coverage is judged per BMC: if one of a location's gateways is dark
but the other tested every BMC, the network's status comes from those tested
results, the dark gateway is still listed, and a note says its targets were
covered. A location with BMCs but no gateway in the topology gives UNKNOWN
paths from
.I "(no gateway: <location>)"
and a note, never OK. The summary line
reads, per network,
.IR "ok/tested paths ok, N untested (K source(s) unreachable)" .
.PP
Every host and interface name placed in the probe script has already passed
the hostname check applied when the topology is loaded and to each
.B \-\-node
name, and is shell-quoted as well. A
.B \-\-node
value that is not a DNS name or an IPv4/IPv6 literal (whitespace, quotes,
shell metacharacters, a leading
.BR \- )
is refused with exit status 2.
.PP
The probes run on the nodes, so they use those nodes' own
.BR ping (8)
dialect. The jumbo-frame probe uses the iputils-only
.BR \-M " and " \-s
options and is valid because this phase always executes on a gateway, never on
the operator's workstation.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
is accepted except
.BR \-\-phase ,
which is fixed to
.BR network .
.SS Phase 3 control
.TP
.B \-\-include\-failed
Probe nodes that failed an earlier phase. By default they are left out of
the node-to-node networks
.RB ( "origin: nodes" ),
because probing a node already known to be down costs a full timeout per pair
and tells the operator nothing new. They are never left out of an
.B "origin: gateways"
network: their BMCs are still probed from the gateways, since a BMC does not
depend on the host OS and those are the BMCs the operator needs after an
outage.
.SS Run control
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
Label for this run, shown in the report header and the logbook subject.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Accepted, and has no effect: this phase issues no power command.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Contact nothing at all; answer every command from the built-in script.
.TP
.BI \-\-location " NAME"
Limit the probe to one location:
.BR mc2 ", " mc1 " or " teststand .
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-node " HOST"
Limit the probe to these nodes. Repeatable; short or fully-qualified names are
both accepted. The mesh is then built over the named nodes only, which is the
quick way to test one suspect path.
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
Accepted; it governs phase 2 and has no effect here.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " STAGE" ", " \-\-until " STAGE"
Accepted; they bound the phase-2 power-on sequence and have no effect here.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.BI \-\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal for ordinary logins. The probes are issued over SSH, so this
phase needs a working login to every source node.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal with root access on the nodes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never prompt for a password; fail with instructions instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault\-addr " URL"
HashiCorp Vault address.
.SS Report
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where the report pages are written. Default:
.IR html/ .
.TP
.B \-\-publish
Copy the report to
.I report.publish.target
afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-target " DEST"
Override the publication target. Implies
.BR \-\-publish .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write the HTML pages; console output only.
.TP
.B \-\-post\-ecl
Accepted; the logbook entry is written by phase 4. See
.BR mu2e-power-report (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-id " N"
Accepted; only phase 4 reads it.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.I mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BI \-\-database\-url " URL"
SQLAlchemy URL for the run store.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
Skip the phase-0 check for a newer revision.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
Print every registered check and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-nodes
Print the node inventory and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Reserve stdout for exactly one JSON document; every human-readable line
(banner, mode notice, phase tables, notes, report and self-update messages, and
a self-update rebuild's output) goes to stderr. See
.B JSON OUTPUT
in
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging, including one line per credential attempted against each source
node.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.SH NOTES
This phase can be the first thing a session runs, which means it can start with
no gateway session cached. Gateway selection is serialised for that reason: the
first worker thread that needs a gateway probes for one and the rest wait,
rather than sixteen threads each opening a TCP sweep and a full SSH handshake
against the same two hosts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every probed path works. Packet loss or a path that cannot carry a full jumbo
frame is a warning and still exits 0.
.TP
.B 1
At least one path failed (FAIL), or at least one path could not be tested
(UNKNOWN).
.TP
.B 2
The probe could not start, or a
.B \-\-node
name is not a valid hostname.
.I run.dry_run: false
in configuration does
.I not
exit 2 here: this command cannot issue a power command, so it always runs as
a dry run, whatever the configuration,
.B \-\-execute
or
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
say (an info line in the log notes what was ignored). The live-run gate
applies to invocations that include phase 2
(see
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR "LIVE RUNS" ).
Another run holds the run lock
.RI ( run.lock_file )
also exits 2, naming the holder.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-netcheck
mu2e-power-netcheck \-\-location teststand
mu2e-power-netcheck \-\-include\-failed \-v
mu2e-power-netcheck \-\-node mu2e-trk-03 \-\-node mu2e-dl-01 \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2e-power-on (1),
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5),
.BR mu2e-probe (1)
